\fs96 \cf2 Calling Mojo from Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you have an existing Python project that would benefit from Mojo's high-performance computing, you shouldn't have to rewrite the whole thing in Mojo. Instead, you can write just the performance-critical parts your code in Mojo and then call it from Python.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf12 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb13 Beta feature\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb13 Calling Mojo code from Python is in early development. You should expect a lot of changes to the API and ergonomics. Likewise, this documentation is still a work in progress. See below for \ul known limitations\ulnone .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Import a Mojo module in Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To illustrate what calling Mojo from Python looks like, we'll start with a simple example, and then dig into the details of how it works and what is possible today.\
Consider a project with the following structure:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb15 project\cb1 \
\cb15 \uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  \u55357 \u56333  main.py\cb1 \
\cb15 \uc0\u9492 \u9472 \u9472  \u55357 \u56613  mojo_module.mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The main entrypoint is a Python program called 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 main.py
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and the Mojo code includes functions to call from Python.\
For example, let's say we want a Mojo function to take a Python value as an argument:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  mojo_module.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf14  \cf20 factorial\cf5 (\cf14 py_obj\cf5 :\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 )\cf14  raises \cf5 ->\cf14  Python\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb15     \cf5 var\cf14  n \cf5 =\cf14  Int\cf5 (\cf14 py\cf5 =\cf14 py_obj\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 return\cf14  math\cf5 .\cf14 factorial\cf5 (\cf14 n\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 And we want to call it from Python like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 \uc0\u55357 \u56333  main.py\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 import\cf14  mojo_module\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf14 mojo_module\cf5 .\cf14 factorial\cf5 (\cf21 5\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 However, before we can call the Mojo function from Python, we must declare it so Python knows it exists.\
Because Python is trying to load 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mojo_module
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , it looks for a function called 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 PyInit_mojo_module()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . (If our file was called 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 foo.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the function Python looked for would be 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 PyInit_foo()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .) Within the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 PyInit_mojo_module()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , we must declare all Mojo functions and types that are callable from Python using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/bindings/PythonModuleBuilder/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul \ulc5 PythonModuleBuilder}}.\
So the complete Mojo code looks like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  mojo_module.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 python \cf5 import\cf14  PythonObject\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 python\cf5 .\cf14 bindings \cf5 import\cf14  PythonModuleBuilder\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 import\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 math\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 os \cf5 import\cf14  abort\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 @export\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf14  \cf20 PyInit_mojo_module\cf5 ()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb15     \cf5 try:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 var\cf14  m \cf5 =\cf14  PythonModuleBuilder\cf5 (\cf22 "mojo_module"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15         m\cf5 .\cf14 def_function\cf5 [\cf14 factorial\cf5 ](\cf22 "factorial"\cf5 ,\cf14  docstring\cf5 =\cf22 "Compute n!"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 return\cf14  m\cf5 .\cf14 finalize\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 except\cf14  e\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15         abort\cf5 (\cf14 String\cf5 (\cf22 "error creating Python Mojo module:"\cf5 ,\cf14  e\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf14  \cf20 factorial\cf5 (\cf14 py_obj\cf5 :\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 )\cf14  raises \cf5 ->\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb15     \cf6 # Raises an exception if `py_obj` is not convertible to a Mojo `Int`.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf14  n \cf5 =\cf14  Int\cf5 (\cf14 py\cf5 =\cf14 py_obj\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 return\cf14  math\cf5 .\cf14 factorial\cf5 (\cf14 n\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 On the Python side, we add the directory containing 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mojo_module.mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to the Python path, and then use a normal 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 import
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement to load our Mojo code:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 \uc0\u55357 \u56333  main.py\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 import\cf14  mojo\cf5 .\cf14 importer\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 import\cf14  mojo_module\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 print(\cf14 mojo_module\cf5 .\cf14 factorial\cf5 (\cf21 5\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 That's it! Try it:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb15 python main.py\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb15 120\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 How it works\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Python supports a standard mechanism called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.python.org/3/extending/extending.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Python extension modules}} that enables compiled languages (like Mojo, C, C++, or Rust) to make themselves callable from Python in an intuitive way. Concretely, a Python extension module is simply a dynamic library that defines a suitable 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 PyInit_*()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function.\
Mojo comes with built-in functionality for defining Python extension modules. The special stuff happens in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mojo.importer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  module we imported.\
If we have a look at the filesystem after Python imports the Mojo code, we'll notice there's a new 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __mojocache__
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  directory, with a dynamic library (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 .so
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) file inside:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb15 project\cb1 \
\cb15 \uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  main.py\cb1 \
\cb15 \uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  mojo_module.mojo\cb1 \
\cb15 \uc0\u9492 \u9472 \u9472  __mojocache__\cb1 \
\cb15     \uc0\u9492 \u9472 \u9472  mojo_module.hash-ABC123.so\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Loading 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 mojo.importer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loads our Python Mojo {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.python.org/3/reference/import.html#import-hooks"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 import hook}}, which behind the scenes looks for a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 .mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  file that matches the imported module name, and if found, compiles it using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/cli/build/#--emit-file_type"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul \ulc5 mojo build --emit shared-lib}} to generate a dynamic library. The resulting file is stored in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 __mojocache__
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and is rebuilt only when it becomes stale (typically, when the Mojo source file changes).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb23 Clearing cached build artifacts\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb23 The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 __mojocache__
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23  directory should contain only derived artifacts. It is always safe to delete the contents of a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 __mojocache__
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23  directory. Needed artifacts will simply be rebuilt the next time the Mojo module is imported.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Now that we've looked at the basics of how Mojo can be used from Python, let's dig into the available features and how you can leverage them to accelerate your Python with Mojo.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Bindings features\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Binding Mojo types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can bind any Mojo type for use in Python using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/bindings/PythonModuleBuilder/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul \ulc5 PythonModuleBuilder}}. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf14  \cf20 Person\cf5 (\cf14 Movable\cf5 ,\cf14  Writable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb15     \cf5 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb15 @export\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf14  \cf20 PyInit_person_module\cf5 ()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb15     \cf5 try:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 var\cf14  mb \cf5 =\cf14  PythonModuleBuilder\cf5 (\cf22 "person_module"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 var\cf14  person_type \cf5 =\cf14  mb\cf5 .\cf14 add_type\cf5 [\cf14 Person\cf5 ](\cf22 "Person"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 except\cf14  e\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15         abort\cf5 (\cf22 "error creating Mojo module"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When you call {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/bindings/PythonModuleBuilder/#add_type"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul \ulc5 add_type()}}, it returns a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/bindings/PythonTypeBuilder/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul \ulc5 PythonTypeBuilder}}, which you can then use to bind the type constructor (see \cf5 \ul \ulc5 binding Python initializers\cf2 \ulnone , below) and methods.\
Any Mojo type bound using a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 PythonTypeBuilder
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  has the resulting Python 'type' object globally registered, enabling two features:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Constructing Python objects that wrap Mojo values for use from Python using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 PythonObject(alloc=Person(..))
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Downcasting using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 python_obj.downcast_value_ptr[Person]()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb23 Mojo types must implement {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/repr/Representable/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb24 \ul Representable}} to be bound for use in Python. Additional traits are required for specific binding features: 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 Movable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23  for custom initializers (
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 def_py_init
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23 ), and both 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 Defaultable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23  and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 Movable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23  for default initializers (
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 def_init_defaultable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23 ).\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 However, merely binding a Mojo type to a Python 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 type
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  object isn't very useful on its own. Next, we'll tell Python how to interact with our Mojo type\'97starting with how to construct instances of our Mojo type from within Python.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Constructing Mojo objects in Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo types can be constructed from Python by declaring a Mojo constructor function as a Python-compatible object initializer using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/bindings/PythonTypeBuilder/#def_py_init"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul def_py_init()}} when you add the type to your module. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 @export\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf14  \cf20 PyInit_person_module\cf5 ()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 try:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15         \cf5 var\cf14  mb \cf5 =\cf14  PythonModuleBuilder\cf5 (\cf22 "person_module"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb25         _ \cf5 =\cf14  mb\cf5 .\cf14 add_type\cf5 [\cf14 Person\cf5 ](\cf22 "Person"\cf5 ).\cf14 def_py_init\cf5 [\cf14 Person\cf5 .\cf14 py_init\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb15         \cf5 return\cf14  mb\cf5 .\cf14 finalize\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 except\cf14  e\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15         abort\cf5 (\cf14 String\cf5 (\cf22 "error creating Python Mojo module:"\cf5 ,\cf14  e\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb15 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 struct\cf14  \cf20 Person\cf5 (\cf14 Movable\cf5 ,\cf14  Writable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb25     \cf5 @staticmethod\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb25     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf20 py_init\cf5 (\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb25         \cf5 out\cf14  self\cf5 :\cf14  Person\cf5 ,\cf14  args\cf5 :\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 ,\cf14  kwargs\cf5 :\cf14  PythonObject\cb1 \
\cb25     \cf5 )\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb25         \cf6 # Validate argument count\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb25         \cf5 if\cf14  \cf20 len\cf5 (\cf14 args\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 !=\cf14  \cf21 2\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb25             \cf5 raise\cf14  Error\cf5 (\cf22 "Person() takes exactly 2 arguments"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb25         \cf6 # Convert Python arguments to Mojo types\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb25         \cf5 var\cf14  name \cf5 =\cf14  String\cf5 (\cf14 args\cf5 [\cf21 0\cf5 ])\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb25         \cf5 var\cf14  age \cf5 =\cf14  Int\cf5 (\cf14 args\cf5 [\cf21 1\cf5 ])\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb25         self \cf5 =\cf14  Self\cf5 (\cf14 name\cf5 ,\cf14  age\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 With this Mojo binding, you can create 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Person
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instances in Python:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 \uc0\u55357 \u56333  Python\
