\fs28 \cf5 \cb23 "Constructor" vs "Initializer"\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb23 In Python, object construction happens across both the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 __new__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23  and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 __init__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23  methods, so the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 __init__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23  method is technically just the attribute initializer. However, in a Mojo struct, there's no 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 __new__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23  method, so we prefer to always call 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 __init__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23  the constructor.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Returning Mojo objects to Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo functions called from Python don't just need to be able to accept {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python_object/PythonObject/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul PythonObject}} values as arguments, they also need to be able to return new values. And sometimes, they even need to be able to return Mojo native values back to Python. This is possible by using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 PythonObject(alloc=<value>)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor.\
An example of this looks like:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf14  \cf20 create_person\cf5 ()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf14  person \cf5 =\cf14  Person\cf5 (\cf22 "Sarah"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf21 32\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 return\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 (\cf14 alloc\cf5 =\cf14 person\cf5 ^)\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf12 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb13 Caution\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb24 PythonObject(alloc=...)
\fs28 \cf2 \cb13  will raise an exception if the provided Mojo object type had not previously been registered using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/bindings/PythonModuleBuilder/#add_type"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb24 \ul PythonModuleBuilder.add_type()}}.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs42 \cf10 \cb18 PythonObject
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  to Mojo values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Within any Mojo code that is handling a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python_object/PythonObject/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul PythonObject}}, but especially within Mojo functions called from Python, it's common to expect an argument of a particular type.\
There are two ways in which a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can be turned into a native Mojo value:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Converting a Python object into a newly constructed Mojo value that has the same logical value as the original Python object. This is handled by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/mojo/std/python/conversions/ConvertibleFromPython/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul ConvertibleFromPython}} trait.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Downcasting a Python object that holds a native Mojo value to a pointer to that inner value. This is handled by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/mojo/std/python/python_object/PythonObject/#downcast_value_ptr"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul PythonObject.downcast_value_ptr()}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs35 \cf10 \cb18 PythonObject
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1  conversions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Many Mojo types support conversion directly from equivalent Python types, via the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/mojo/std/python/conversions/ConvertibleFromPython/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul ConvertibleFromPython}} trait:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb15 # Given a person, clone them and give them a different name.\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb15 def\cf14  \cf20 create_person\cf5 (\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     name_obj\cf5 :\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 ,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     age_obj\cf5 :\cf14  PythonObject\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb15 )\cf14  raises \cf5 ->\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf6 # These conversions will raise an exception if they fail\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf14  name \cf5 =\cf14  String\cf5 (\cf14 name_obj\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     \cf5 var\cf14  age \cf5 =\cf14  Int\cf5 (\cf14 age_obj\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb15     \cf5 return\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 (\cf14 alloc\cf5 =\cf14 Person\cf5 (\cf14 name\cf5 ,\cf14  age\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Which could be called from Python using:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 \uc0\u55357 \u56333  Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb15 person \cf5 =\cf14  mojo_module\cf5 .\cf14 create_person\cf5 (\cf22 "John Smith"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

