\fs64 \cf2 Strategies for porting Python to Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Writing Pythonic code in Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In this approach to bindings, we embrace the flexibility of Python, and eschew trying to convert 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  arguments into the narrowly constrained, strongly-typed space of the Mojo type system, in favor of just writing some code and letting it raise an exception at runtime if we got something wrong.\
The flexibility of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  enables a unique programming style, wherein Python code can be "ported" to Mojo with relatively few changes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 \uc0\u55357 \u56333  Python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf14  \cf20 foo\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 ,\cf14  y\cf5 ,\cf14  z\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     x\cf5 [\cf14 y\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  \cf20 int\cf5 (\cf14 z\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     x \cf5 =\cf14  y \cf5 +\cf14  z\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Rule of thumb: Any Python builtin function should be accessible in Mojo using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 Python.<builtin>()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb15 def\cf14  \cf20 foo\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 ,\cf14  y\cf5 :\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 ,\cf14  z\cf5 :\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  PythonObject\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     x\cf5 [\cf14 y\cf5 ]\cf14  \cf5 =\cf14  Python\cf5 .\cf20 int\cf5 (\cf14 z\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb15     x \cf5 =\cf14  y \cf5 +\cf14  z\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Building Mojo extension modules\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can create and distribute your Mojo modules for Python in the following ways:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
As source files, compiled on demand using the Python Mojo importer hook.\uc0\u8232 The advantage of this approach is that it's easy to get started with, and keeps your project structure simple, while ensuring that your imported Mojo code is always up to date after you make an edit.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
As pre-built Python extension module 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 .so
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  dynamic libraries, compiled using:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf14 \cb15 mojo build mojo_module.mojo \cf21 --emit\cf14  shared-lib \cf21 -o\cf14  mojo_module.so\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb15 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cb15 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \cb1 This has the advantage that you can specify any other necessary build options manually (optimization or debug flags, import paths, etc.), providing an "escape hatch" from the Mojo import hook abstraction for advanced users.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Known limitations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 While we have big ambitions for Python to Mojo interoperability\'97our goal is for Mojo to be the best way to extend Python\'97this feature is still in early and active development, and there are some limitations to be aware of. These will be lifted over time.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Functions taking more than 6 arguments. Currently 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 PyTypeBuilder.add_function()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and related function bindings only support Mojo functions that take up to 6 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  arguments: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 def(PythonObject, PythonObject, PythonObject, PythonObject, PythonObject, PythonObject)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Keyword arguments syntax. Currently, Mojo functions called from Python only accept keyword arguments when using a trailing 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 kwargs: OwnedKwargsDict[PythonObject]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument. Support for native 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb18 **kwargs
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  syntax will be added in the future.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo package dependencies. Mojo code that has dependencies on packages other than the Mojo stdlib (like those in the ever-growing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/modular-community"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Modular Community}} package channel) are currently only supported when building Mojo extension modules manually, as the Mojo import hook does not currently support a way to specify import paths for Mojo package dependencies.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Properties. Computed properties getter and setters are not currently supported.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Expected type conversions. A handful of Mojo standard library types can be constructed directly from equivalent Python builtin object types, by implementing the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/mojo/std/python/conversions/ConvertibleFromPython/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 \ul ConvertibleFromPython}} trait. However, many Mojo standard library types do not yet implement this trait, so may require manual conversion logic if needed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri960\partightenfactor0
